<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px 0; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Goals</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Personal Goals for the Future Include...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow audio narratives. Interactive: Engage in role-play exercises to resolve conflicts.
            <br><br>
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow audio narratives about educational goals and demonstrate understanding by paraphrasing key ideas and intentions (e.g., "What does Carlos want to achieve?" "He wants to get a degree in engineering").
            Can engage in role-play exercises about future education, showing comprehension by following dialogue steps (e.g., "What will Ana study next?" "Science").
            Can answer simple questions about peers' plans, demonstrating understanding of future education topics (e.g., "What is Carlos' goal?" "To be a teacher").
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing images of different future professions (e.g., engineer, doctor, artist, business person) and writes "What do you want to be in the future?" on the board. Students listen as the teacher says a few common goals and raises hands if they hear one they like. Then, the teacher says simple statements like "I want to achieve my goals," "I plan to study hard for my career." Students listen for keywords like 'future', 'goals', 'plan', 'career' and repeat them. (Focus: Listening for key words, activating prior knowledge, introducing theme vocabulary.)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher clearly states and models the pronunciation of core vocabulary: 'future', 'goals', 'plan', 'business', 'career', 'opportunity', 'vision', 'development', 'achieve', 'prepare', 'set', 'ambitious', 'important', 'future-oriented'. Teacher also models future tense structures (e.g., "I will study hard.") and modals ("You have to work hard."). The teacher explicitly models the minimal pair /θ/ in "math" and /s/ in "mass," asking students to listen and repeat the difference. (Focus: Accurate pronunciation and introduction of target vocabulary and grammar structures for listening recognition.)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary and concepts.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'future' about yesterday or tomorrow?" (Expected answer: "Tomorrow!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are 'goals' things you want to achieve or things you don't care about?" (Expected answer: "Things you want to achieve!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I have to study,' does it mean it's optional or necessary?" (Expected answer: "Necessary!")</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio narrative (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) titled "Three Students, Three Dreams." The narrative features three 8th-grade students (e.g., Ana, Carlos, Sofia) discussing their future plans and goals after high school, incorporating modals of necessity ("have to," "must"), WH-questions for future goals, future continuous ("will be working"), and target vocabulary (e.g., 'business', 'career', 'opportunity', 'development', 'ambitious', 'prepare'). The register is informal, conversational, suitable for adolescents. (Genre: Dialogue/Short narrative; Format: Audio recording.)</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can follow audio narratives about educational goals and demonstrate understanding by paraphrasing key ideas and intentions. Pragmatic: Discussing goals: "I want to go to university after high school." Responding to questions: "What are your plans after graduation?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> Students listen to the audio once and choose the main idea from two options presented on the board (e.g., A. "The students are talking about their favorite food." B. "The students are talking about their future goals and careers.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listen):</b> Students listen to the audio a second time. The teacher provides 3-4 simple True/False statements related to the audio's content. For example: "Carlos wants to study engineering." (True) "Ana plans to open a business right after high school." (False) "Sofia thinks working hard is important for her future." (True).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening: Can follow audio narratives about educational goals. Grammar: Modals of necessity "have to", "must". WH-questions for educational and career goals. Vocabulary: 'future', 'goals', 'plan', 'business', 'career', 'opportunity', 'achieve', 'prepare', 'important'. Pronunciation: Focus on future tense structures and modals, minimal pairs /θ/ and /s/ in context.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap Fill (Listening for specific words):</b> The teacher distributes a short transcript of a section of the audio with 5-6 blanks. Students listen to the audio (a specific segment) again and fill in the missing target vocabulary words or grammatical structures (e.g., "I have to ______ hard to achieve my ______. In five years, I will be ______ as an engineer."). Students then compare answers in pairs.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Listen and Identify:</b> The teacher plays sentences from the audio that contain "have to" or "will be + -ing." After each sentence, students raise one finger if they hear "have to/must" (necessity) and two fingers if they hear "will be + -ing" (future continuous). For pronunciation focus, the teacher selects sentences with minimal pairs /θ/ and /s/ (e.g., "Ana likes math, so she has to pass the test," contrasting 'math' with a made-up word 'mass' if needed, or by contrasting 'think' with 'sink'). Students listen and identify the sound.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Listening and Paraphrasing:</b> In pairs, students take turns listening to short sentences (teacher-provided) about future plans (e.g., "My goal is to prepare for a career in business.") and paraphrase what they heard to their partner (e.g., "Your partner wants to work in business."). The partner confirms if the paraphrasing is correct, focusing on listening for comprehension and intention.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play / Peer Interview – "My Future Vision."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Student A describes their personal goals for the future using target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "I want to achieve my dream of working in business. I have to study hard. In five years, I will be developing new opportunities.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B's role is to actively listen, ask at least three WH-questions to understand and clarify Student A's plans (e.g., "What will you study?", "When do you plan to start?", "Where do you see yourself in ten years?"), and offer encouragement (e.g., "You have to work hard to achieve your goals!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After 3-4 minutes, they switch roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This activity focuses on active listening, asking relevant questions, and responding appropriately, demonstrating understanding of future plans. The 'role-play to resolve conflicts' aspect of the specific objective can be interpreted as one student listening to a peer's perceived challenge (e.g., "I'm worried about the math I have to study for engineering") and the other offering verbal encouragement and potential solutions.</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes student pairs during the role-play/interview in Stage 4. Students will also complete a short listening check.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a checklist to note students' ability to:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrate understanding of audio narratives by correctly answering comprehension questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ask relevant WH-questions to understand peers' future plans.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use target vocabulary (e.g., 'future', 'goals', 'career', 'opportunity', 'achieve') and grammar ('have to', future continuous) in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Offer simple encouragement or feedback based on active listening.</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new goal you heard about today from a classmate or in the audio?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word or phrase about future plans that you learned or practiced today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and teacher reflection)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I listen to my classmates' future plans today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to discussions about future goals was easy/difficult for me?"</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students effectively follow the audio narrative and demonstrate understanding through paraphrasing and answering questions?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were the target vocabulary and grammatical structures ('have to', future continuous, WH-questions) sufficiently addressed and understood in a listening context?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolding are needed for students who struggled with listening comprehension or active listening during the role-play?"</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will move from listening to reading comprehension. They will read a short text or article about young people's future goals, using the same core vocabulary and grammatical structures introduced in this listening lesson, to deepen their understanding of the theme.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>